  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **radius of its base**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the radius of its base.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kr,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and using the fact that the height of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi rh\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =kr \\\\ 2\\pi rh\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =kr \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{kr}{2\\pi rh} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{2\\pi h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi h}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K.\n$$",
